1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:02,362
Williams Obstetrics.

2
00:00:02,312 --> 00:00:05,387
Chapter 19: Fetal Therapy.

3
00:00:05,387 --> 00:00:22,125
Medical Therapy Surgical Therapy Open Fetal Surgery Fetoscopic Surgery Percutaneous Procedures Ex-Utero Intrapartum Treatment References Innovative treatments developed during the past three decades have dramatically altered the course of selected fetal anomalies and conditions.

4
00:00:22,125 --> 00:00:29,100
Over time, fetal interventions have become less invasive, and the number of fetal centers has expanded.

5
00:00:29,100 --> 00:00:36,337
The North American Fetal Therapy Network now includes 36 medical centers in the United States and Canada.

6
00:00:36,337 --> 00:00:43,387
Some of the fetal abnormalities and conditions amenable to either medical or surgical treatment are presented here.

7
00:00:43,387 --> 00:00:54,175
The management of fetal anemia and thrombocytopenia is reviewed in Chapter 18, and treatment of some fetal infections is discussed in Chapters 67 and 68.

8
00:00:54,175 --> 00:00:56,175
Medical Therapy.

9
00:00:56,175 --> 00:01:05,137
Selected medications administered to the pregnant woman are transported across the placenta in concentrations high enough to treat fetal conditions.

10
00:01:05,137 --> 00:01:12,550
Pregnancy physiology affects drug concentration, and transfer depends on maternal and placental metabolism .

11
00:01:12,550 --> 00:01:14,837
# Arrhythmias.

12
00:01:14,837 --> 00:01:32,037
Abnormal fetal cardiac rhythms are grouped into three categories: tachyarrhythmias, heart rates >180 beats per minute (bpm); bradyarrhythmias, heart rates <110 bpm; and ectopy, typically premature atrial contractions.

13
00:01:32,037 --> 00:01:49,525
If a fetal arrhythmia is suspected, M-mode ultrasound, described in Chapter 15 (p. 294), is used to measure the atrial and ventricular rates and to clarify the relationship between atrial and ventricular beats, thereby diagnosing the type of rhythm disturbance.

14
00:01:49,525 --> 00:01:59,937
Premature Atrial Contractions If the fetal heart rate is normal but the rhythm is irregular, the most common etiology is premature atrial contractions (PACs).

15
00:01:59,937 --> 00:02:10,037
These atrial ectopic beats are found in 1 to 2 percent of uncomplicated pregnancies (Hahurij, 2011; Strasburger, 2010).

16
00:02:10,037 --> 00:02:14,300
PACs represent immaturity of the cardiac conduction system.

17
00:02:14,300 --> 00:02:18,937
They typically resolve later in gestation or in the neonatal period.

18
00:02:18,937 --> 00:02:28,600
PACs are usually an isolated finding but may be associated with redundancy of the foramen ovale flap—formerly termed a foramen ovale aneurysm.

19
00:02:28,600 --> 00:02:33,412
When a PAC is conducted, an extra beat is heard with handheld Doppler.

20
00:02:33,412 --> 00:02:40,987
However, the premature contraction more commonly arrives at the atrioventricular node during the refractory period.

21
00:02:40,987 --> 00:02:45,837
This results in a compensatory pause, which sounds like a dropped beat.

22
00:02:45,837 --> 00:02:53,012
M-mode evaluation confirms the diagnosis (Fig. 15-46, p. 295).

23
00:02:53,012 --> 00:03:01,862
PACs may occur as frequently as every other beat, which means that the auscultated fetal heart rate may be as low as 60 to 80 bpm.

24
00:03:01,862 --> 00:03:07,887
Known as blocked atrial bigeminy, this condition is benign and does not require treatment .

25
00:03:07,887 --> 00:03:14,187
However, monitoring the fetus in labor may be challenging and necessitate cesarean delivery.

26
00:03:14,187 --> 00:03:22,825
M-mode ultrasound will differentiate atrial bigeminy from other causes of bradycardia, such as thirddegree atrioventricular block.

27
00:03:22,825 --> 00:03:32,350
Up to 2 percent of fetuses with PACs are later found to have supraventricular tachycardia (Copel, 2000; Srinivasan, 2008).

28
00:03:32,350 --> 00:03:45,150
Given the importance of prompt identification and treatment of supraventricular tachyarrhythmias, pregnancies with PACs are often monitored with fetal heart rate assessment every 1 to 2 weeks until the ectopy resolves.

29
00:03:45,150 --> 00:03:51,787
At Parkland Hospital, we find that auscultation with handheld Doppler is sufficient for surveillance.

30
00:03:51,787 --> 00:03:59,762
Tachyarrhythmias The two most common tachyarrhythmias are supraventricular tachycardia (SVT) and atrial flutter.

31
00:03:59,762 --> 00:04:09,825
SVT is characterized by an abrupt increase in the fetal heart rate to 180 to 300 bpm with 1:1 atrioventricular concordance .

32
00:04:09,825 --> 00:04:14,050
The typical range is 200 to 240 bpm.

33
00:04:14,050 --> 00:04:22,912
SVT may develop secondary to an ectopic focus or to an accessory atrioventricular pathway leading to a reentrant tachycardia.

34
00:04:22,912 --> 00:04:32,962
Atrial flutter is characterized by a much higher atrial rate, generally 300 to 500 bpm, with varying degrees of atrioventricular block.

35
00:04:32,962 --> 00:04:41,700
As a result, the ventricular rate in a fetus with atrial flutter may range from below normal to approximately 250 bpm .

36
00:04:41,700 --> 00:04:48,037
In contrast, fetal sinus tachycardia typically presents with a gradual heart rate rise

37
00:04:48,196 --> 00:04:51,471
to a rate that is only slightly above normal.

38
00:04:51,421 --> 00:04:59,933
With this, readily discernible causes may be maternal fever or hyperthyroidism, or rarely, fetal anemia or infection.

39
00:04:59,933 --> 00:05:02,346
Figure 19-1.

40
00:05:02,346 --> 00:05:05,996
Supraventricular tachycardia (SVT).

41
00:05:05,996 --> 00:05:13,746
This M-mode image at 20 weeks’ gestation demonstrates an initially normal fetal heartrate of 150 bpm.

42
00:05:13,746 --> 00:05:20,396
Midway through the image (arrow), the fetal heart rate suddenly increases to 240 bpm.

43
00:05:20,396 --> 00:05:26,408
With SVT, there is one atrial beat (A) for each ventricular beat (V).

44
00:05:26,408 --> 00:05:28,833
Figure 19-2.

45
00:05:28,833 --> 00:05:30,671
Atrial flutter.

46
00:05:30,671 --> 00:05:39,421
In this M-mode image at 32 weeks’ gestation, calipers mark the ventricular rate, which is approximately 220 bpm.

47
00:05:39,421 --> 00:05:50,971
There are two atrial beats (A) for each ventricular beat (V), such that the atrial rate is approximately 440 bpm with 2:1 atrioventricular block.

48
00:05:50,971 --> 00:06:00,158
If a fetal tachyarrhythmia is identified, it is important to determine whether it is sustained—defined as present for at least 50 percent of the time.

49
00:06:00,158 --> 00:06:09,208
It may be necessary to monitor the fetal heart rate for 12 to 24 hours upon initial detection, and then periodically to reassess .

50
00:06:09,208 --> 00:06:14,421
Unsustained or intermittent tachyarrhythmias may become sustained over time.

51
00:06:14,421 --> 00:06:21,433
Although intermittent tachyarrhythmias do not generally require treatment, close fetal surveillance is warranted.

52
00:06:21,433 --> 00:06:33,221
Sustained fetal tachyarrhythmias with ventricular rates exceeding 200 bpm impair ventricular filling to a degree that the risks for developing cardiomyopathy and hydrops are significant.

53
00:06:33,221 --> 00:06:40,183
With atrial flutter, lack of coordinated atrioventricular contractions may further compound this risk.

54
00:06:40,183 --> 00:06:50,033
Maternal administration of antiarrhythmic agents that cross the placenta may convert the rhythm to normal or may lower the baseline heart rate to forestall heart failure.

55
00:06:50,033 --> 00:06:55,358
Therapy can require dosages at the upper end of the therapeutic adult range.

56
00:06:55,358 --> 00:07:00,808
Therefore, a maternal electrocardiogram is obtained before and during therapy.

57
00:07:00,808 --> 00:07:07,921
Monitoring of the maternal serum level may be necessary, particularly if the dosage requires titration.

58
00:07:07,921 --> 00:07:13,858
If medical therapy is successful, the medication is generally continued until delivery.

59
00:07:13,858 --> 00:07:20,408
Antiarrhythmic medications most commonly used include digoxin, flecainide, and sotalol.

60
00:07:20,408 --> 00:07:26,171
Their selection depends on the type of tachyarrhythmia and provider experience with the drug.

61
00:07:26,171 --> 00:07:34,033
Traditionally, digoxin was the initial preferred treatment, but its placental transfer may be poor in the setting of hydrops.

62
00:07:34,033 --> 00:07:43,458
Many centers now use flecainide or sotalol as first-line therapy (Ekiz, 2018; van der Heijden, 2013).

63
00:07:43,458 --> 00:07:52,771
A second agent is needed in more than 50 percent of cases (Jaeggi, 2011; O’Leary, 2020; Shah, 2012).

64
00:07:52,771 --> 00:08:06,708
With treatment, conversion to a normal rhythm or reduction in heart rate to a normal range occurs in 90 percent, including 80 percent of those with hydrops (Miyoshi, 2019; Ueda, 2018).

65
00:08:06,708 --> 00:08:11,646
SVT is more likely than atrial flutter to convert to a normal rhythm.

66
00:08:11,646 --> 00:08:23,883
The neonatal survival rate exceeds 90 percent (Ekman-Joelsson, 2015; Miyoshi, 2019; O’Leary, 2020; van der Heijden, 2013).

67
00:08:23,883 --> 00:08:29,871
Bradyarrhythmia The most common etiology of fetal bradycardia is congenital heart block.

68
00:08:29,871 --> 00:08:37,708
Approximately 50 percent of cases occur in the setting of a structural cardiac abnormality involving the conduction system.

69
00:08:37,708 --> 00:08:48,258
These include heterotaxy, in particular left-atrial isomerism; endocardial cushion defect; and less commonly corrected transposition of the great vessels .

70
00:08:48,258 --> 00:08:59,958
The prognosis of heart block from a structural cardiac anomaly is extremely poor, and fetal loss rates exceed 80 percent (Glatz, 2008; Strasburger, 2010).

71
00:08:59,958 --> 00:09:12,808
In a structurally normal heart, 85 percent of atrioventricular block cases are caused by transplacental passage of maternal anti-SSA/Ro or antiSSB/La antibodies .

72
00:09:12,808 --> 00:09:20,321
Many of these women have, or subsequently develop, systemic lupus erythematosus or other autoimmune disease .

73
00:09:20,321 --> 00:09:26,521
The risk of third-degree heart block with Ro antibodies is small—only approximately 2 percent.

74
00:09:26,521 --> 00:09:32,021
However, the risk may reach 20 percent if a prior infant has been affected.

75
00:09:32,021 --> 00:09:43,396
Immune-mediated congenital heart block confers a mortality rate of nearly 20 percent, requires permanent pacing in two thirds of surviving children, and also poses a risk for

76
00:09:43,564 --> 00:09:49,789
cardiomyopathy (Brito-Zeron, 2015; Izmirly, 2011).

77
00:09:49,739 --> 00:09:59,326
If associated with effusions, bradyarrhythmias, or endocardial fibroelastosis, neonatal status may progressively worsen after birth.

78
00:09:59,326 --> 00:10:06,551
Research has focused on maternal corticosteroid therapy to potentially reverse fetal heart block or to forestall it.

79
00:10:06,551 --> 00:10:23,951
In the PR Interval and Dexamethasone (PRIDE) study, a multicenter trial of pregnancies with antiSSA/Ro antibodies, Friedman and colleagues (2008, 2009) performed weekly fetal echocardiography to assess for development of heart block.

80
00:10:23,951 --> 00:10:29,651
Fetal heart block was treated with maternal oral dexamethasone, 4 mg daily.

81
00:10:29,651 --> 00:10:38,464
Unfortunately, therapy did not prevent progression from second- to thirddegree block, and third-degree atrioventricular block was irreversible.

82
00:10:38,464 --> 00:10:44,839
In rare cases, there was a potential benefit in reversing first-degree atrioventricular block.

83
00:10:44,839 --> 00:10:50,164
However, first-degree block did not generally progress even without treatment.

84
00:10:50,164 --> 00:11:04,714
Similarly, a subsequent review of pregnancies with isolated second- or third-degree fetal heart block found that dexamethasone therapy did not affect disease progression, need for pacemaker in the neonatal period, or overall survival rates .

85
00:11:04,714 --> 00:11:18,664
Despite considerable enthusiasm for corticosteroid treatment, a systematic review of more than 700 pregnancies with treated fetal heart block demonstrated no improvement in fetal or neonatal morbidity or mortality rates .

86
00:11:18,664 --> 00:11:23,401
Thus, we do not recommend dexamethasone use for this indication.

87
00:11:23,401 --> 00:11:33,039
More recent efforts have turned to potential therapy with hydroxychloroquine (Plaquenil), a mainstay of treatment for systemic lupus erythematosus .

88
00:11:33,039 --> 00:11:44,714
In a review of more than 250 women with prior affected children, early treatment with hydroxychloroquine was associated with a significant decrease in recurrence of congenital heart block .

89
00:11:44,714 --> 00:11:54,101
A subsequent clinical trial found that treatment with hydroxychloroquine was associated with a greater than 50-percent reduction in the rate of fetal heart block .

90
00:11:54,101 --> 00:12:02,139
In each of these series, fewer than 8 percent of children from pregnancies treated with hydroxychloroquine experienced heart block.

91
00:12:02,139 --> 00:12:05,039
Research in this area is ongoing.

92
00:12:05,039 --> 00:12:16,576
Maternal terbutaline has also been given in small cohorts with either alloimmune- or heterotaxy-mediated fetal heart block in which the fetal heart rate is persistently below 56 bpm.

93
00:12:16,576 --> 00:12:23,464
The terbutaline dosage is titrated to a maternal heart rate of 95 to 115 bpm.

94
00:12:23,464 --> 00:12:35,264
Fetal heart rate increases of 5 to 10 bpm have been reported, but resolution of hydrops has not been consistently demonstrated (Cuneo, 2007, 2010).

95
00:12:35,264 --> 00:12:38,726
# Congenital Adrenal Hyperplasia.

96
00:12:38,726 --> 00:12:50,464
Several autosomal recessive enzyme deficiencies impair fetal synthesis of cortisol from cholesterol by the adrenal cortex, leading to congenital adrenal hyperplasia (CAH).

97
00:12:50,464 --> 00:12:58,339
CAH is the most common etiology of androgen excess in those with 46,XX disorders of sex development .

98
00:12:58,339 --> 00:13:12,289
Lack of cortisol stimulates adrenocorticotrophic hormone (ACTH) secretion by the anterior pituitary, overproduction of androstenedione and testosterone, and subsequent virilization of female fetuses.

99
00:13:12,289 --> 00:13:25,964
Sequelae may include formation of labioscrotal folds, persistence of a urogenital sinus, or even creation of a penile urethra and scrotal sac (Fig. 15-55, p. 299).

100
00:13:25,964 --> 00:13:32,214
More than 90 percent of CAH cases are caused by 21-hydroxylase deficiency.

101
00:13:32,214 --> 00:13:35,801
There are two types: classic and nonclassic.

102
00:13:35,801 --> 00:13:44,114
The incidence of classic CAH approximates 1 in 15,000 births worldwide but is higher in selected populations.

103
00:13:44,114 --> 00:13:49,239
Among Yupik Eskimos, the reported incidence is 1 in 300 births .

104
00:13:49,239 --> 00:14:07,014
Of those with classic CAH, 75 percent require postnatal treatment with mineralocorticoids and glucocorticoids to prevent a salt-wasting adrenal crisis, which is characterized by hyponatremia, dehydration, hypotension, and even cardiovascular collapse.

105
00:14:07,014 --> 00:14:15,476
The remaining 25 percent with classic CAH have the simple virilizing type and also require glucocorticoid supplementation.

106
00:14:15,476 --> 00:14:21,889
Nonclassic CAH may present with precocious pubarche, hirsutism, or infertility.

107
00:14:21,889 --> 00:14:26,451
However, affected individuals also may be asymptomatic.

108
00:14:26,451 --> 00:14:32,776
The prevalence of nonclassic CAH approximates 1 case per 200 Caucasians and

109
00:14:32,932 --> 00:14:36,132
Ashkenazi Jews in the United States .

110
00:14:36,082 --> 00:14:43,757
As discussed in Chapter 32 (p.594), all states mandate newborn screening for CAH.

111
00:14:43,757 --> 00:14:58,944
For more than three decades, dexamethasone has been administered to the pregnant woman to suppress fetal androgen overproduction and either obviate or ameliorate virilization of female fetuses (David, 1984; New, 2012).

112
00:14:58,944 --> 00:15:06,007
Prenatal corticosteroid therapy is successful in most cases if initiated early and taken consistently.

113
00:15:06,007 --> 00:15:12,144
One metaanalysis found that dexamethasone treatment was associated with reduced virilization.

114
00:15:12,144 --> 00:15:20,694
Specifically, the Prader score, which grades genital masculinization on a scale 1 to 5, improved by 2.3 grades .

115
00:15:20,694 --> 00:15:31,569
The alternative is consideration of postnatal genitoplasty, which may include clitoroplasty, urogenital sinus surgery, and additional vaginoplasty procedures.

116
00:15:31,569 --> 00:15:42,132
In a recent review, 18 percent of children treated with feminizing genitoplasty experienced postoperative complications, and 12 percent required further surgery .

117
00:15:42,132 --> 00:15:54,369
The typical preventive regimen is oral dexamethasone given to the mother at a dosage of 20 μg/kg/d—up to 1.5 mg per day, divided in three doses.

118
00:15:54,369 --> 00:16:07,019
The critical period for external genitalia development is 7 to 12 weeks’ gestation, and treatment to prevent virilization should be initiated by 9 weeks—before it is known whether the fetus is affected.

119
00:16:07,019 --> 00:16:14,432
Because this is an autosomal recessive condition, affected females make up only 1 in 8 atrisk conceptions.

120
00:16:14,432 --> 00:16:19,557
Carrier parents are typically identified after the birth of an affected child.

121
00:16:19,557 --> 00:16:31,982
Molecular genetic testing is clinically available in such cases and initially uses sequence analysis of the CYP21A2 gene, which encodes the 21hydroxylase enzyme .

122
00:16:31,982 --> 00:16:42,294
If this is uninformative, gene-targeted deletion/duplication analysis is performed, and additional testing such as whole exome sequencing may be considered .

123
00:16:42,294 --> 00:16:49,194
A goal of prenatal diagnosis is to limit dexamethasone exposure in males and in unaffected females.

124
00:16:49,194 --> 00:17:01,832
If both parents are determined to be carriers, prenatal molecular genetic testing may be performed on chorionic villi—at 10 to 12 weeks’ gestation—or on amniocytes after 15 weeks.

125
00:17:01,832 --> 00:17:09,982
Determination of fetal gender using cell-free DNA (cfDNA) may aid in avoiding dexamethasone treatment.

126
00:17:09,982 --> 00:17:22,357
CfDNA sensitivity to detect Y-chromosome sequences is at least 95 percent when performed at or beyond 7 weeks (Devaney, 2011; Hill, 2011).

127
00:17:22,357 --> 00:17:34,344
In the research setting, effective cfDNA testing using hybridization probes flanking the CYP21A2 gene has been reported as early as 56/7 weeks’ gestation .

128
00:17:34,344 --> 00:17:38,719
Maternal treatment with dexamethasone has become controversial.

129
00:17:38,719 --> 00:17:45,144
The Endocrine Society recommends that treatment be given only in the context of research protocols .

130
00:17:45,144 --> 00:17:53,582
It further recommends that such protocols incorporate cfDNA screening for the Y-chromosome to avoid treatment of male fetuses.

131
00:17:53,582 --> 00:18:07,632
Of note, if therapy is initiated shortly before 9 weeks’ gestation, the dose of dexamethasone used is not considered to have significant teratogenic potential because organogenesis of major organs has already taken place .

132
00:18:07,632 --> 00:18:16,932
Ongoing concerns focus on the potential effects of either excess endogenous androgens or excess exogenous dexamethasone on the developing brain.

133
00:18:16,932 --> 00:18:27,282
Although maternal dexamethasone has been used for many years to prevent virilization of female fetuses with CAH, long-term safety data are relatively limited.

134
00:18:27,282 --> 00:18:31,507
# Congenital Cystic Adenomatoid Malformation.

135
00:18:31,507 --> 00:18:42,632
This well-circumscribed lung mass may appear solid and echogenic or may have one or multiple variably sized cysts (Fig. 15-33, p. 288).

136
00:18:42,632 --> 00:18:52,882
Lesions with one or more cysts ≥5 mm are termed macrocystic, whereas solid lesions and those with smaller cysts are microcystic .

137
00:18:52,882 --> 00:19:04,557
A small subset of microcystic congenital cystic adenomatoid malformations (CCAMs) may demonstrate rapid growth, generally between 18 and 26 weeks’ gestation.

138
00:19:04,557 --> 00:19:13,744
The mass may become so large that it causes mediastinal shift, which may compromise cardiac output and venous return, leading to hydrops .

139
00:19:13,744 --> 00:19:21,882
A CCAM-volume ratio (CVR) has been used to quantify size and risk for hydrops in these severe cases .

140
00:19:21,882 --> 00:19:25,344
This ratio is an estimate of the CCAM

141
00:19:25,516 --> 00:19:34,528
volume using the formula for a prolate ellipse (length × width × height × π/6) divided by the head circumference.

142
00:19:34,478 --> 00:19:48,141
In a series of 40 pregnancies with microcystic CCAM, the mean CVR was 0.5 at 20 weeks’ gestation, peaked at 1.0 by 26 weeks, and then declined sharply before delivery .

143
00:19:48,141 --> 00:19:54,416
With a CVR exceeding 1.6, the risk for hydrops is as high as 60 percent.

144
00:19:54,416 --> 00:20:08,491
However, if the initial CVR is below 1.6, CCAM growth resulting in hydrops develops in fewer than 2 percent of cases (Ehrenberg-Buchner, 2013; Peranteau, 2016).

145
00:20:08,491 --> 00:20:13,316
A CVR threshold of 1.0 also may assist counseling.

146
00:20:13,316 --> 00:20:25,291
In a series of 62 pregnancies with fetal lung masses, a maximal CVR >1.0 was associated with a 75-percent likelihood that the neonate would be symptomatic .

147
00:20:25,291 --> 00:20:33,578
However, no fetus with a CVR ≤1.0 subsequently required surgery in the newborn period.

148
00:20:33,578 --> 00:20:41,141
If the CVR exceeds 1.6 or if signs of hydrops develop, corticosteroid treatment may be beneficial.

149
00:20:41,141 --> 00:20:54,778
Regimens include dexamethasone—6.25 mg every 12 hours for four doses, or betamethasone—12.5 mg intramuscularly every 24 hours for two doses.

150
00:20:54,778 --> 00:21:09,341
A single course of corticosteroids has been associated with resolution of hydrops in approximately 80 percent of cases, and 90 percent of treated fetuses survived (Loh, 2012; Peranteau, 2016).

151
00:21:09,341 --> 00:21:26,678
Multiple courses of corticosteroids—generally two—have been advocated for fetuses with large CCAM lesions and with persistent or worsening hydrops or ascites despite a single course of medication (Derderian, 2015; Peranteau, 2016).

152
00:21:26,678 --> 00:21:30,866
Therapy for macrocystic CCAM is discussed later.

153
00:21:30,866 --> 00:21:33,391
# Thyroid Disease.

154
00:21:33,391 --> 00:21:40,778
Identification of fetal thyroid disease is rare and is usually prompted by sonographic detection of a fetal goiter.

155
00:21:40,778 --> 00:21:51,778
If a goiter is identified, determination of fetal hyper- or hypothyroidism is essential, and thyroid hormone levels should be measured in amnionic fluid or fetal blood.

156
00:21:51,778 --> 00:22:03,191
Traditionally, fetal blood sampling was preferred to amniocentesis for guiding treatment, but data are limited (Abuhamad, 1995; Ribault, 2009).

157
00:22:03,191 --> 00:22:09,228
Performance of these procedures is discussed in Chapter 17 (p. 347).

158
00:22:09,228 --> 00:22:15,491
Goals of therapy are correction of the physiological abnormality and diminished goiter size.

159
00:22:15,491 --> 00:22:21,928
The goiter may compress the trachea and esophagus, leading to hydramnios from impaired swallowing.

160
00:22:21,928 --> 00:22:32,228
Despite this, case reports attest to lack of airway compromise and favorable outcomes (Blumenfeld, 2013; Machado, 2019).

161
00:22:32,228 --> 00:22:42,553
Thyrotoxicosis Maternal Graves disease may result in transplacental passage of immunoglobulin G (IgG) thyroid-stimulating antibodies.

162
00:22:42,553 --> 00:23:02,053
Untreated fetal thyrotoxicosis can present with goiter, tachycardia, growth restriction, hydramnios, accelerated bone maturation, and even heart failure and hydrops (Huel, 2009; Kiefer, 2017; Peleg, 2002; van Dijk, 2018).

163
00:23:02,053 --> 00:23:10,378
Fetal blood sampling may be considered to confirm the diagnosis (Duncombe, 2001; Srisupundit, 2008).

164
00:23:10,378 --> 00:23:20,241
Maternal administration of antithyroid medication may be needed, even if the woman has had prior surgery or ablation and no longer has hyperthyroidism.

165
00:23:20,241 --> 00:23:26,553
If the pregnant women develops hypothyroidism, she is treated with supplemental levothyroxine .

166
00:23:26,553 --> 00:23:37,103
Hypothyroidism In a woman receiving medication for Graves disease, transplacental passage of methimazole or propylthiouracil may cause fetal hypothyroidism .

167
00:23:37,103 --> 00:23:57,053
Other potential causes of fetal hypothyroidism resulting in goiter include transplacental passage of thyroid peroxidase antibodies, fetal thyroid dyshormonogenesis, and maternal consumption of iodine supplements (Agrawal, 2002; Hardley, 2018; Overcash, 2016).

168
00:23:57,053 --> 00:24:04,166
Goitrous hypothyroidism may lead to hydramnios, neck hyperextension, and delayed bone maturation.

169
00:24:04,166 --> 00:24:08,428
Treatment with intraamnionic levothyroxine should be considered.

170
00:24:08,428 --> 00:24:23,003
Optimal dosage and frequency have not been established but have typically ranged from 150 to 500 μg every 1 to 4 weeks (Machado, 2019; Nemescu, 2020; Ribault, 2009).

171
00:24:23,003 --> 00:24:28,941
If the pregnant woman is receiving antithyroid medication, it is generally discontinued.

172
00:24:28,941 --> 00:24:31,078
Surgical Therapy.

173
00:24:31,078 --> 00:24:41,328
Fetal surgery, also called maternal-fetal surgery, is offered for selected congenital abnormalities in which the likelihood of deterioration during gestation

174
00:24:41,500 --> 00:24:49,137
is so great that delaying treatment until after delivery would risk fetal death or substantially greater postnatal morbidity.

175
00:24:49,087 --> 00:24:57,837
Fetal surgical procedures are highly specialized interventions performed at relatively few centers to treat a small number of fetal conditions.

176
00:24:57,837 --> 00:25:03,012
They require extensive preoperative counseling and multidisciplinary care.

177
00:25:03,012 --> 00:25:07,925
Principles for guiding case selection are listed in Table 19-1.

178
00:25:07,925 --> 00:25:14,025
When considering fetal surgery, the overriding concern is the safety of the mother and fetus.

179
00:25:14,025 --> 00:25:18,037
Accomplishing the fetal goals of the procedure is secondary .

180
00:25:18,037 --> 00:25:20,387
Table 19-1.

181
00:25:20,387 --> 00:25:29,575
Guiding Principles for Fetal Surgical Procedures Selected abnormalities amenable to fetal surgical treatment are shown in Table 19-2.

182
00:25:29,575 --> 00:25:39,362
An overview of some of these procedures, their indications, and complications is provided here to assist with initial patient evaluation and counseling.

183
00:25:39,362 --> 00:25:45,712
Additional content is also found in Cunningham and Gilstrap’s Operative Obstetrics, 3rd edition.

184
00:25:45,712 --> 00:25:48,162
TABLE 19-2.

185
00:25:48,162 --> 00:25:55,462
Selected Fetal and Placental Abnormalities Amenable to InUtero Procedures # Open Fetal Surgery.

186
00:25:55,462 --> 00:26:02,725
Fetal procedures are considered open if they are accomplished though a hysterotomy that is not performed for the purpose of delivery.

187
00:26:02,725 --> 00:26:10,687
Open procedures are performed under general endotracheal anesthesia to suppress uterine contractions and fetal responses.

188
00:26:10,687 --> 00:26:19,787
The hysterotomy is made with a stapling device, and intraoperative ultrasound is used to avoid the placental edge and to verify fetal position.

189
00:26:19,787 --> 00:26:27,475
The stapler seals the edges of the myometrium and membranes to achieve hemostasis and avoid chorioamnion separation.

190
00:26:27,475 --> 00:26:34,737
Warmed fluid is continuously infused into the uterus thorough a rapid infusion device to limit cord compression.

191
00:26:34,737 --> 00:26:45,850
The fetus is gently manipulated to facilitate exposure, to permit pulse oximetry monitoring, and to establish venous access in case fluids or blood are emergently needed.

192
00:26:45,850 --> 00:26:50,900
The surgical procedure is then performed, and the hysterotomy is closed.

193
00:26:50,900 --> 00:26:56,100
Prophylactic antibiotics are generally administered for 24 hours.

194
00:26:56,100 --> 00:27:04,425
Tocolysis typically includes intravenous magnesium sulfate for 24 hours and oral indomethacin for 48 hours.

195
00:27:04,425 --> 00:27:09,787
Cesarean delivery is needed later in gestation and for all future deliveries.

196
00:27:09,787 --> 00:27:15,075
Risks Open fetal surgery entails significant maternal and fetal risks.

197
00:27:15,075 --> 00:27:22,512
The most recent data are from studies of fetal myelomeningocele repair, which is the most commonly performed procedure.

198
00:27:22,512 --> 00:27:30,087
Morbidities identified in the Management of Myelomeningocele Study (MOMS) are shown in Table 19-3 .

199
00:27:30,087 --> 00:27:41,975
In a review of 26 open fetal myelomeningocele cases, 15 percent experienced preterm rupture of membranes, and the mean gestational age at delivery was 35 weeks .

200
00:27:41,975 --> 00:27:50,537
Other potential risks include maternal sepsis and fetal death during or following the procedure, particularly if hydrops is present.

201
00:27:50,537 --> 00:28:04,125
Wilson and associates (2010) reviewed subsequent pregnancy outcomes following open fetal surgery and reported that 14 percent of women experienced uterine rupture and 14 percent had uterine dehiscence.

202
00:28:04,125 --> 00:28:06,587
Table 19-3.

203
00:28:06,587 --> 00:28:19,650
Benefits and Risks of Fetal Myelomeningocele Surgery versus Postnatal Repair Myelomeningocele Surgery Fetal myelomeningocele is the first nonlethal birth defect for which in-utero repair has been offered .

204
00:28:19,650 --> 00:28:34,537
Following standard postnatal myelomeningocele repair, affected children experience varying degrees of paralysis, bladder and bowel dysfunction, developmental delays, and brainstem dysfunction from the Arnold-Chiari II malformation .

205
00:28:34,537 --> 00:28:49,162
Damage is postulated to result from abnormal embryonic neurulation followed by exposure of neural elements to amnionic fluid throughout pregnancy (Adzick, 2010; Meuli, 1995, 1997).

206
00:28:49,162 --> 00:28:51,575
Figure 19-3.

207
00:28:51,575 --> 00:28:54,475
Fetal myelomeningocele surgery.

208
00:28:54,475 --> 00:29:02,262
A. With the edges of both the laparotomy and hysterotomy incisions retracted, the skin around the defect is incised.

209
00:29:02,262 --> 00:29:07,875
Subsequently, the neural placode is sharply dissected from the arachnoid membrane.

210
00:29:07,875 --> 00:29:15,262
B. The dural membrane is reflected to the midline to cover the neural placode and is reapproximated using suture.

211
00:29:15,262 --> 00:29:18,887
In some cases a patch is needed (not shown).

212
00:29:18,887 --> 00:29:22,425
The fetal skin incision is subsequently sutured.

213
00:29:22,425 --> 00:29:25,075
Last, hysterotomy and

214
00:29:25,228 --> 00:29:27,840
laparotomy are then closed.

215
00:29:27,790 --> 00:29:40,178
Figures 19-3, 19-4, 19-6, and 19-8: Reproduced with permission from Shamshirsaz AA, Ramin, SM, Belfort MA: Fetal therapy.

216
00:29:40,178 --> 00:29:49,003
In Yeomans ER, Hoffman BL, Gilstrap LC III, et al: Cunningham and Gilstrap’s Operative Obstetrics, 3rd ed.

217
00:29:49,003 --> 00:29:53,440
New York, McGraw Hill Education, 2017.

218
00:29:53,440 --> 00:30:05,878
In the landmark MOMS trial, Adzick and colleagues (2011) randomized 183 pregnancies to prenatal or standard postnatal myelomeningocele repair at three centers.

219
00:30:05,878 --> 00:30:30,615
Criteria for trial participation included: (1) a singleton fetus at 19.0 to 25.9 weeks’ gestation; (2) an upper myelomeningocele boundary between T1 and S1 confirmed by magnetic resonance imaging; (3) evidence of hindbrain herniation; and (4) a normal karyotype and no evidence of a fetal anomaly unrelated to the myelomeningocele.

220
00:30:30,615 --> 00:30:44,140
Women at risk for preterm birth or placental abruption, those with a contraindication to fetal surgery, and women with body mass index (BMI) >35 kg/m2 were excluded.

221
00:30:44,140 --> 00:30:50,490
The MOMS trial demonstrated improved early childhood outcomes in the prenatal surgery cohort .

222
00:30:50,490 --> 00:30:56,815
Children who had undergone fetal surgery were twice as likely to walk independently by 30 months.

223
00:30:56,815 --> 00:31:05,240
They had significantly less hindbrain herniation and were only half as likely to undergo ventriculoperitoneal shunting by age 1 year.

224
00:31:05,240 --> 00:31:15,953
A primary outcome was a composite score that was derived from the Bayley Mental Development Index and from the difference between the functional and anatomical level of the lesion at 30 months.

225
00:31:15,953 --> 00:31:21,390
This primary outcome was also significantly better in the prenatal surgery group.

226
00:31:21,390 --> 00:31:31,715
Despite these benefits, most children who received fetal surgery were not able to ambulate independently, and nearly 30 percent were not able to ambulate at all.

227
00:31:31,715 --> 00:31:40,690
Prenatal surgery did not confer improvements in fetal or neonatal death rates or in the Bayley Mental Development Index score at age 30 months.

228
00:31:40,690 --> 00:31:47,840
Prenatal surgery was also associated with a small risk for placental abruption and maternal pulmonary edema.

229
00:31:47,840 --> 00:31:56,040
Moreover, nearly half were delivered before 34 weeks, which significantly increased the risk for respiratory distress syndrome .

230
00:31:56,040 --> 00:32:09,315
It is the position of the American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2017) that nondirective counseling include the option of fetal surgery for all pregnancies that meet MOMS trial criteria.

231
00:32:09,315 --> 00:32:24,165
Study of fetal myelomeningocele repair in women with BMI up to 40 kg/m2 has demonstrated outcomes similar to those in women of lower BMI (Hilton, 2019; Moldenhauer, 2020).

232
00:32:24,165 --> 00:32:28,615
Otherwise, MOMS trial criteria are strictly followed.

233
00:32:28,615 --> 00:32:35,553
Outcomes of children aged 6 to 10 years who previously participated in the MOMs trial are now available .

234
00:32:35,553 --> 00:32:41,353
Fetal surgery conferred a sustained benefit in the likelihood of independent ambulation.

235
00:32:41,353 --> 00:32:50,790
Nearly 30 percent of children were able to ambulate independently, and approximately 50 percent required ventriculoperitoneal shunt placement.

236
00:32:50,790 --> 00:32:54,853
Overall cognitive function was similar between cohorts .

237
00:32:54,853 --> 00:33:00,528
Thoracic Masses Open fetal surgery is rarely performed for thoracic masses.

238
00:33:00,528 --> 00:33:04,203
Most are small, and these have a good prognosis.

239
00:33:04,203 --> 00:33:10,540
Large CCAMs are often treated medically with a course of corticosteroids (p. 370).

240
00:33:10,540 --> 00:33:17,778
A dominant cyst in a CCAM may be amenable to drainage or shunt placement (p. 376).

241
00:33:17,778 --> 00:33:26,165
Similarly, an isolated pleural effusion surrounding a pulmonary sequestration may be amenable to drainage or shunt placement.

242
00:33:26,165 --> 00:33:37,165
Fetal surgery is generally reserved for pregnancies prior to 32 weeks’ gestation in which there is a large, solid-appearing or microcystic mass and hydrops is developing.

243
00:33:37,165 --> 00:33:44,115
In carefully selected cases, the survival rate following open lobectomy approximates 60 percent .

244
00:33:44,115 --> 00:33:51,503
Use of the ex-utero intrapartum treatment procedure in the treatment of fetal lung masses at delivery is discussed later.

245
00:33:51,503 --> 00:34:07,028
Sacrococcygeal Teratoma The perinatal mortality rate for cases of sacrococcygeal teratoma diagnosed prenatally is 20 to 40 percent (Hedrick, 2004; Shue, 2013, Simonini, 2021).

246
00:34:07,028 --> 00:34:18,465
Poor prognostic factors include a solid component constituting more than 50 percent of the tumor mass and a tumor volume-to-fetal weight ratio exceeding 12 percent prior to 24

247
00:34:18,628 --> 00:34:20,665
weeks’ gestation .

248
00:34:20,615 --> 00:34:32,503
Hydramnios is common, and hydrops may develop from high-output cardiac failure, either as a consequence of tumor vascularity or secondary to bleeding within the tumor and resulting anemia.

249
00:34:32,503 --> 00:34:36,828
Fetal loss rates approach 100 percent in such cases.

250
00:34:36,828 --> 00:34:43,815
Mirror syndrome—maternal preeclampsia developing along with fetal hydrops—may occur in this setting .

251
00:34:43,815 --> 00:34:53,415
Open fetal surgery is considered only if the tumor is completely external and high cardiac output with early hydrops has developed in the second trimester .

252
00:34:53,415 --> 00:35:03,753
However, given the poor prognosis, rapid growth beyond 27 weeks often prompts early delivery and postnatal resection rather than open fetal surgery .

253
00:35:03,753 --> 00:35:06,590
# Fetoscopic Surgery.

254
00:35:06,590 --> 00:35:12,778
As with open fetal surgeries, these procedures are performed at highly specialized centers.

255
00:35:12,778 --> 00:35:19,190
Fetoscopy is performed with fiberoptic endoscopes that measure 1 to 2 mm in diameter.

256
00:35:19,190 --> 00:35:25,753
Instruments such as lasers fit through a 3- to 5-mm cannula that surrounds the endoscope.

257
00:35:25,753 --> 00:35:32,490
Not all fetoscopic procedures involve placing the instruments through the maternal anterior abdominal wall.

258
00:35:32,490 --> 00:35:40,178
In some cases, laparotomy facilitates optimal placement and maneuvering of the instruments or positioning of the fetus.

259
00:35:40,178 --> 00:35:45,840
Examples of conditions treated by fetoscopy are listed in Table 19-2.

260
00:35:45,840 --> 00:36:00,278
Twin-Twin Transfusion Syndrome As discussed in Chapter 48 (p. 849), fetoscopic laser ablation of placental anastomoses is the preferred management for severe twin-twin transfusion syndrome (TTTS).

261
00:36:00,278 --> 00:36:16,715
It is generally performed between 16 and 26 weeks’ gestation for monochorionic-diamnionic twin pregnancies with stage II to stage IV TTTS (Quintero, 1999; Society for Maternal-Fetal Medicine, 2013).

262
00:36:16,715 --> 00:36:22,915
A fetoscope inserted into the sac of the recipient twin is used to image the vascular equator.

263
00:36:22,915 --> 00:36:28,290
The vascular equator separates the placental cotyledons that supply each twin .

264
00:36:28,290 --> 00:36:35,765
Selective laser photocoagulation involves individually coagulating anastomoses that cross between the twins .

265
00:36:35,765 --> 00:36:53,228
Arteriovenous anastomoses along the placental surface of the equator are visualized with the fetoscope and then photocoagulated using a 600-μm diameter diode laser or a 400-μm neodymium:yttrium-aluminum-garnet (Nd:YAG) laser .

266
00:36:53,228 --> 00:36:57,778
The procedure is typically performed under epidural analgesia.

267
00:36:57,778 --> 00:37:09,228
At the end, amnioreduction is performed to decrease the single deepest pocket of amnionic fluid to below 5 cm, and antibiotics are injected into the amnionic cavity.

268
00:37:09,228 --> 00:37:11,690
Figure 19-4.

269
00:37:11,690 --> 00:37:16,690
Selective laser photocoagulation for twin-twin transfusion syndrome.

270
00:37:16,690 --> 00:37:26,140
The fetoscope is inserted into the recipient-twin sac and positioned over the vascular equator, which lies in between the two placental cord insertion sites.

271
00:37:26,140 --> 00:37:33,303
Arteriovenous anastomoses along the placental surface are individually photocoagulated using the laser.

272
00:37:33,303 --> 00:37:40,528
Reproduced with permission from Shamshirsaz AA, Ramin, SM, Belfort MA: Fetal therapy.

273
00:37:40,528 --> 00:37:49,353
In Yeomans ER, Hoffman BL, Gilstrap LC III, et al: Cunningham and Gilstrap’s Operative Obstetrics, 3rd ed.

274
00:37:49,353 --> 00:37:53,840
New York, NY: McGraw Hill; 2017.

275
00:37:53,840 --> 00:37:56,403
Figure 19-5.

276
00:37:56,403 --> 00:38:02,303
Fetoscopic photograph of laser photocoagulation for twin-twin transfusion syndrome.

277
00:38:02,303 --> 00:38:08,815
A. Vascular anastomoses (arrows) are shown before photocoagulation is performed.

278
00:38:08,815 --> 00:38:13,928
B. The ablation sites appear as blanched yellow-white areas (arrows).

279
00:38:13,928 --> 00:38:18,403
Reproduced with permission from Dr. Timothy M. Crombleholme.

280
00:38:18,403 --> 00:38:29,990
Unfortunately, residual anastomoses remain in up to a third of cases and may lead to TTTS recurrence or to the development of twin anemiapolycythemia sequence (TAPS).

281
00:38:29,990 --> 00:38:39,053
The latter is a feto-fetal transfusion characterized by large differences in hemoglobin concentrations between a pair of monochorionic twins.

282
00:38:39,053 --> 00:38:50,103
With the Solomon technique, immediately after selective photocoagulation, the laser is used to coagulate the entire vascular equator from one edge of the placenta to the other .

283
00:38:50,103 --> 00:39:02,240
Placental dye-injection studies confirm a significant reduction in the number of residual anastomoses following this procedure (Ruano, 2013; Slaghekke, 2014b).

284
00:39:02,240 --> 00:39:08,715
Families should have reasonable expectations of procedural success and potential complications.

285
00:39:08,715 --> 00:39:12,915
Without treatment, the perinatal mortality rate for severe

286
00:39:13,084 --> 00:39:16,584
TTTS is 70 to 100 percent.

287
00:39:16,534 --> 00:39:27,271
Following laser therapy, the perinatal mortality rate approximates 30 to 50 percent, and the risk for long-term neurological handicap is 5 to 20 percent .

288
00:39:27,271 --> 00:39:36,134
One series reported a double-twin survival rate of nearly 70 percent and survival of at least one twin in more than 90 percent of cases .

289
00:39:36,134 --> 00:39:47,384
Ischemic fetal brain lesions have been identified in 2 percent of those treated by laser and include cystic periventricular leukomalacia and grade III or IV interventricular hemorrhage .

290
00:39:47,384 --> 00:39:52,346
Cerebral palsy has been reported in 5 percent of surviving children .

291
00:39:52,346 --> 00:40:09,334
Procedure-related complications include preterm prelabor ruptured membranes in up to 25 percent, placental abruption in 8 percent, vascular laceration in 3 percent, and amnionic band syndrome resulting from laser laceration of the membranes in 3 percent.

292
00:40:09,334 --> 00:40:24,934
Additionally, TAPS complicates 16 percent of pregnancies treated with selective laser photocoagulation and 3 percent treated with the Solomon technique (Habli, 2009; Robyr, 2006; Slaghekke, 2014b).

293
00:40:24,934 --> 00:40:31,234
The majority of laser-treated TTTS pregnancies deliver before 34 weeks’ gestation .

294
00:40:31,234 --> 00:40:42,259
Congenital Diaphragmatic Hernia Early attempts to treat congenital diaphragmatic hernia (CDH) used open fetal surgery to reposition the liver into the abdomen.

295
00:40:42,259 --> 00:40:47,071
This unfortunately kinked the umbilical vein and led to fetal demise .

296
00:40:47,071 --> 00:40:57,084
Knowledge that lungs normally produce fluid and that fetuses with upper airway obstruction develop pulmonary hyperplasia formed the rationale for tracheal occlusion.

297
00:40:57,084 --> 00:41:00,584
The idea was to “plug the lung until it grows” .

298
00:41:00,584 --> 00:41:05,221
Initial efforts focused on occluding the trachea with an external clip .

299
00:41:05,221 --> 00:41:13,871
Subsequently, a detachable silicone or latex balloon was placed within the trachea endoscopically and inflated with normal saline .

300
00:41:13,871 --> 00:41:26,684
This procedure, fetoscopic tracheal occlusion (FETO), is offered to selected pregnancies with isolated CDH in which the prognosis is otherwise poor based on the degree of fetal liver herniation.

301
00:41:26,684 --> 00:41:29,209
Figure 19-6.

302
00:41:29,209 --> 00:41:32,509
Fetoscopic tracheal occlusion (FETO).

303
00:41:32,509 --> 00:41:37,509
The endoscope enters the fetal oropharynx and advances down the trachea.

304
00:41:37,509 --> 00:41:43,421
Inset: The balloon is inflated to occlude the trachea, and then the endoscope is removed.

305
00:41:43,421 --> 00:41:50,646
Reproduced with permission from Shamshirsaz AA, Ramin, SM, Belfort MA: Fetal therapy.

306
00:41:50,646 --> 00:41:59,471
In Yeomans ER, Hoffman BL, Gilstrap LC III, et al: Cunningham and Gilstrap’s Operative Obstetrics, 3rd ed.

307
00:41:59,471 --> 00:42:03,959
New York, NY: McGraw Hill; 2017.

308
00:42:03,959 --> 00:42:09,984
FETO uses a 3-mm operating sheath and fetoscopes as small as 1 mm .

309
00:42:09,984 --> 00:42:15,459
The procedure is generally performed between 26 and 30 weeks’ gestation.

310
00:42:15,459 --> 00:42:24,884
The balloon is removed after 6 weeks or at approximately 34 weeks, either through a second fetoscopic procedure or by ultrasound-guided puncture .

311
00:42:24,884 --> 00:42:35,984
Fetal lamb research demonstrated that without balloon removal, the number of type II pneumocytes was markedly reduced, but that balloon removal normalized type II pneumocyte density .

312
00:42:35,984 --> 00:42:42,334
Because the procedure is performed fetoscopically, vaginal delivery is not contraindicated.

313
00:42:42,334 --> 00:42:55,959
In 2003, a randomized trial of the FETO procedure in pregnancies with isolated CDH, liver herniation, and lung-to-head ratio <1.4 did not identify a benefit from fetal therapy .

314
00:42:55,959 --> 00:43:02,971
Survival rates 90 days after birth were unexpectedly high—75 percent—in both groups.

315
00:43:02,971 --> 00:43:10,234
Therefore, the lung-tohead threshold was lowered and adjusted for gestational age in an effort to improve prediction.

316
00:43:10,234 --> 00:43:18,959
In a metaanalysis of more than 200 pregnancies, treatment with FETO was associated with a 13-fold improvement in survival rates .

317
00:43:18,959 --> 00:43:24,721
FETO has also been associated with improved survival rates in right-sided CDH .

318
00:43:24,721 --> 00:43:32,584
Although study outcomes are promising, this procedure is currently available in the United States only through a research protocol.

319
00:43:32,584 --> 00:43:34,771
Lung-to-Head Ratio.

320
00:43:34,771 --> 00:43:44,721
This imaging index was developed to improve prediction of survival in fetuses with isolated left-sided CDH diagnosed before 25 weeks’ gestation .

321
00:43:44,721 --> 00:43:52,734
The lung-to-head ratio (LHR) is a semi-quantitative estimate of the right lung area divided by the head circumference.

322
00:43:52,734 --> 00:44:01,071
Investigators found that the neonatal survival rate was 100 percent if the LHR was >1.35 and that

323
00:44:01,228 --> 00:44:06,103
there were no survivors if the LHR was <0.6 .

324
00:44:06,053 --> 00:44:18,253
Nearly three fourths of pregnancies had values between 0.6 and 1.35, and prediction was difficult in this group because the overall survival rate approximated 60 percent.

325
00:44:18,253 --> 00:44:23,665
Modifications to the LHR have been developed in an effort to improve prediction.

326
00:44:23,665 --> 00:44:34,940
Jani and colleagues (2007) derived an observed-to-expected (O/E) LHR nomogram to account for differential growth of the head and torso across gestation.

327
00:44:34,940 --> 00:44:55,403
Lung area has been measured in 3 different ways: (1) by tracing the lung circumference, (2) by multiplying the longest diameter of the lung with its longest perpendicular diameter, and (3) by multiplying the anterior-posterior diameter of the lung at the mid-clavicular line by the perpendicular diameter at its midpoint .

328
00:44:55,403 --> 00:45:07,890
The North American Fetal Therapy Network found that reproducibility of the O/E LHR was highest when the lung circumference was traced, but that overall interrater agreement was lower than anticipated .

329
00:45:07,890 --> 00:45:23,665
Fetoscopic Myelomeningocele Repair After publication of the MOMS trial findings, research efforts focused on whether maternal morbidities associated with open fetal myelomeningocele repair might be mitigated if the procedure were accomplished fetoscopically.

330
00:45:23,665 --> 00:45:34,753
Araujo Junior and associates (2016) conducted a systematic review that included 456 open cases and 84 fetoscopic surgeries.

331
00:45:34,753 --> 00:45:46,340
The endoscopic procedures were generally performed by inserting instruments through the maternal abdominal wall and then through the uterine wall, with partial carbon dioxide insufflation of the uterus.

332
00:45:46,340 --> 00:45:56,390
The rate of maternal myometrial dehiscence or attenuation was only 1 percent following endoscopy compared with 26 percent following open procedures.

333
00:45:56,390 --> 00:46:09,540
However, fetoscopy was associated with significantly higher rates of preterm delivery before 34 weeks—80 versus 45 percent, and of perinatal mortality—14 versus 5 percent.

334
00:46:09,540 --> 00:46:18,903
More recently, investigators have performed fetoscopic myelomeningocele repair but used laparotomy and exteriorization of the uterus .

335
00:46:18,903 --> 00:46:34,178
The proportion of infants requiring ventriculoperitoneal shunts before age 1 year—approximately 40 percent— was similar to that with open fetal surgery in the MOMS trial (Adzick, 2011; Belfort, 2017).

336
00:46:34,178 --> 00:46:46,178
In one series of 34 pregnancies treated with open fetoscopic myelomeningocele repair, the median gestational age at delivery was 38 weeks, and 50 percent delivered vaginally .

337
00:46:46,178 --> 00:46:49,065
Research is ongoing in this area.

338
00:46:49,065 --> 00:46:52,140
# Percutaneous Procedures.

339
00:46:52,140 --> 00:47:00,403
These procedures are performed using a shunt, angioplasty catheter, radiofrequency ablation needle, or bipolar cautery.

340
00:47:00,403 --> 00:47:10,365
Under ultrasound guidance, instruments are inserted through the maternal abdominal wall, uterine wall, and membranes to reach the amnionic cavity and fetus.

341
00:47:10,365 --> 00:47:20,128
Risks may include preterm labor, preterm prelabor ruptured membranes, placental abruption, maternal infection, and fetal injury or loss.

342
00:47:20,128 --> 00:47:25,615
Thoracic Shunts These shunts drain fetal pleural fluid into the amnionic cavity .

343
00:47:25,615 --> 00:47:33,790
A large effusion may result in pulmonary hypoplasia or may cause mediastinal shift that is severe enough to result in hydrops.

344
00:47:33,790 --> 00:47:40,990
The most common etiology of a primary effusion is chylothorax, which is caused by lymphatic obstruction.

345
00:47:40,990 --> 00:47:51,465
Chylothorax is diagnosed if a cell count performed on the pleural fluid demonstrates that more than 80 percent of the cells are lymphocytes, and there is no evidence of infection.

346
00:47:51,465 --> 00:48:02,190
Pleural effusions may also form secondary to congenital viral infection or aneuploidy, or they may be associated with a malformation such as a pulmonary sequestration.

347
00:48:02,190 --> 00:48:11,590
Yinon and associates (2010) reported aneuploidy in approximately 5 percent and associated anomalies in 10 percent of cases.

348
00:48:11,590 --> 00:48:14,065
Figure 19-7.

349
00:48:14,065 --> 00:48:16,978
Thoracoamnionic shunt placement.

350
00:48:16,978 --> 00:48:25,153
A. A large, right-sided fetal pleural effusion (asterisks) and ascites were identified at 18 weeks’ gestation.

351
00:48:25,153 --> 00:48:29,015
The effusion was drained but rapidly reaccumulated.

352
00:48:29,015 --> 00:48:35,615
The xanthochromic fluid contained 95-percent lymphocytes, consistent with chylothorax.

353
00:48:35,615 --> 00:48:41,078
B. A double-pigtail shunt (arrow) was inserted under ultrasound guidance.

354
00:48:41,078 --> 00:48:45,090
Following shunt placement, the effusion and ascites

355
00:48:45,244 --> 00:48:46,869
resolved.

356
00:48:46,819 --> 00:48:51,619
Typically, the effusion is first drained using a 22-gauge needle.

357
00:48:51,619 --> 00:48:58,381
The fluid may be sent for chromosomal microarray analysis, infection studies, and a cell count.

358
00:48:58,381 --> 00:49:05,631
If the effusion recurs, a double-pigtail shunt is placed through the fetal chest wall using a trocar and cannula.

359
00:49:05,631 --> 00:49:13,031
For a right-sided effusion, the shunt is placed in the lower third of the chest to permit maximum expansion of the lung.

360
00:49:13,031 --> 00:49:20,331
If left-sided, the shunt is placed along the upper axillary line to allow the heart to return to normal position.

361
00:49:20,331 --> 00:49:30,769
The overall survival rate is 70 percent, and that for hydropic fetuses approximates 50 percent (Mann, 2010; Yinon, 2010).

362
00:49:30,769 --> 00:49:37,894
We recommend weekly surveillance following shunt placement because displacement into the amnionic cavity is common.

363
00:49:37,894 --> 00:49:44,094
At time of delivery, the shunt must be clamped immediately to avoid neonatal pneumothorax.

364
00:49:44,094 --> 00:49:53,581
A shunt may also be used drain a dominant cyst in a fetus with macrocystic CCAM (Fig. 15-33, p. 288).

365
00:49:53,581 --> 00:49:59,956
Only rarely are such cysts large enough to confer risk for hydrops or pulmonary hypoplasia.

366
00:49:59,956 --> 00:50:08,869
Shunt placement may improve the survival rate to 90 percent in those without hydrops and to at least 75 percent if hydrops has developed .

367
00:50:08,869 --> 00:50:19,006
Urinary Shunts Vesicoamnionic shunts are offered in selected cases of fetal bladder-outlet obstruction in which the amnionic fluid volume is severely diminished.

368
00:50:19,006 --> 00:50:24,794
Lower urinary tract obstruction (LUTO) occurs more often in male fetuses.

369
00:50:24,794 --> 00:50:37,806
It is most commonly caused by posterior urethral valves but may be due to anterior urethral valves, urethral atresia or stenosis, or prune belly syndrome, which is also called Eagle-Barrett syndrome.

370
00:50:37,806 --> 00:50:45,244
Cases in females may be associated with complex cloacal abnormalities or the megacystismicrocolon syndrome.

371
00:50:45,244 --> 00:50:57,231
Ultrasound findings include dilation of the bladder and proximal urethra, termed the “keyhole” sign, along with bladder wall thickening (Fig. 15-61, p. 301).

372
00:50:57,231 --> 00:51:03,319
Associated oligohydramnios before midpregnancy leads to pulmonary hypoplasia.

373
00:51:03,319 --> 00:51:10,006
Unfortunately, postnatal renal function may be poor even when amnionic fluid volume is normal.

374
00:51:10,006 --> 00:51:26,656
Evaluation includes a careful search for associated anomalies, which may occur in 40 percent of cases, and for aneuploidy, which has been reported in 5 to 8 percent (Hayden, 1988; Hobbins, 1984; Mann, 2010).

375
00:51:26,656 --> 00:51:33,081
Potential candidates for therapy are fetuses without other severe anomalies or genetic syndromes.

376
00:51:33,081 --> 00:51:41,056
Therapy is generally offered only if the fetus is male because the underlying anomaly tends to be even more severe in females.

377
00:51:41,056 --> 00:51:52,919
Serial bladder drainage—vesicocentesis—performed under ultrasound guidance at approximately 48-hour intervals is used to evaluate fetal urine electrolyte and protein content.

378
00:51:52,919 --> 00:52:03,681
Fetal urine is normally hypotonic due to tubular resorption of sodium and chloride, whereas isotonic urine in the setting of obstruction suggests renal tubular damage.

379
00:52:03,681 --> 00:52:08,481
Serial assessment has been used to guide candidate selection for therapy .

380
00:52:08,481 --> 00:52:13,556
Chromosomal microarray analysis also may be performed on fetal urine.

381
00:52:13,556 --> 00:52:16,031
Table 19-4.

382
00:52:16,031 --> 00:52:25,844
Fetal Urinary Analyte Values with Bladder Outlet Obstruction Vesicoamnionic shunt placement allows urine to drain from the bladder into the amnionic cavity .

383
00:52:25,844 --> 00:52:35,444
While this may prevent pulmonary hypoplasia, it does not reliably preserve renal function, particularly if cortical cysts are visible sonographically .

384
00:52:35,444 --> 00:52:40,644
Warmed lactated Ringer solution is first infused into the amniotic cavity.

385
00:52:40,644 --> 00:52:48,969
Amnioinfusion improves ultrasound visualization, thereby facilitating evaluation of fetal anatomy and shunt placement.

386
00:52:48,969 --> 00:52:53,606
A small trocar and cannula are then inserted into the fetal bladder.

387
00:52:53,606 --> 00:53:02,794
A double-pigtail catheter is used, and the shunt is placed as caudal as possible within the bladder to avoid dislodgement after bladder decompression.

388
00:53:02,794 --> 00:53:05,156
Figure 19-8.

389
00:53:05,156 --> 00:53:08,094
Vesicoamnionic shunt placement.

390
00:53:08,094 --> 00:53:16,344
A. After amnioinfusion is performed, a trocar is inserted into the distended fetal bladder under sonographic guidance.

391
00:53:16,344 --> 00:53:19,919
The pigtail catheter is threaded into the trocar.

392
00:53:19,919 --> 00:53:26,019
B. The double-pigtail shunt has been deployed down the trocar, and the trocar has been removed.

393
00:53:26,019 --> 00:53:29,494
The distal end of the shunt is coiled within the fetal

394
00:53:29,644 --> 00:53:34,319
bladder, and the proximal end is draining into the amnionic cavity.

395
00:53:34,269 --> 00:53:41,494
Reproduced with permission from Shamshirsaz AA, Ramin, SM, Belfort MA: Fetal therapy.

396
00:53:41,494 --> 00:53:50,319
In Yeomans ER, Hoffman BL, Gilstrap LC III, et al: Cunningham and Gilstrap’s Operative Obstetrics, 3rd ed.

397
00:53:50,319 --> 00:53:54,806
New York, NY: McGraw Hill; 2017.

398
00:53:54,806 --> 00:54:14,231
Complications include displacement of the shunt out of the fetal bladder in up to 40 percent of cases, urinary ascites in approximately 20 percent, and even development of bowel herniation through the abdominal wall defect— gastroschisis—in up to 10 percent (Freedman, 2000; Mann, 2010).

399
00:54:14,231 --> 00:54:24,769
Preterm delivery is common, and reported neonatal survival rates range from 50 to 90 percent (Biard, 2005; Walsh, 2011).

400
00:54:24,769 --> 00:54:33,006
A third of surviving children have required dialysis or renal transplantation, and almost half have respiratory problems .

401
00:54:33,006 --> 00:54:42,856
In a randomized trial comparing vesicoamnionic shunt with conservative management in 31 cases, those receiving shunts had higher survival rates.

402
00:54:42,856 --> 00:54:48,044
However, only two children had normal renal function at age 2 years .

403
00:54:48,044 --> 00:55:02,669
Similarly, a metaanalysis of LUTO studies performed between 1990 and 2015 found that vesicoamnionic shunt conferred perinatal survival benefit but no improvement in renal function or survival at age 2 years .

404
00:55:02,669 --> 00:55:10,569
Radiofrequency Ablation With this procedure, high-frequency alternating current is used to coagulate and desiccate tissue.

405
00:55:10,569 --> 00:55:21,669
Radiofrequency ablation (RFA) is the favored modality for treatment of twin reversed arterial perfusion (TRAP) sequence, also known as acardiac twin .

406
00:55:21,669 --> 00:55:28,781
Without treatment, the mortality rate for the normal or pump twin in severe TRAP sequence exceeds 50 percent.

407
00:55:28,781 --> 00:55:35,244
The procedure is also used for selective termination with other monochorionic twin complications.

408
00:55:35,244 --> 00:55:48,769
Under ultrasound guidance, a 17- to 19-gauge RFA needle is directed into the base of the umbilical cord within the abdomen of the acardiac twin, and a 2-cm area of coagulation is achieved.

409
00:55:48,769 --> 00:55:53,356
Color Doppler is used to verify absent flow into the acardius.

410
00:55:53,356 --> 00:55:57,581
The procedure is generally performed at 20 weeks’ gestation.

411
00:55:57,581 --> 00:56:09,556
The neonatal survival rate of the normal or pump twin approximates 85 percent (Cabassa, 2013; Lee, 2013; Wagata, 2016).

412
00:56:09,556 --> 00:56:16,956
Risks are higher for monoamnionic twin pregnancies, in whom the survival rate is only 67 percent .

413
00:56:16,956 --> 00:56:23,131
The most common complications are preterm prelabor ruptured membranes and preterm birth.

414
00:56:23,131 --> 00:56:29,419
RFA has generally been offered for TRAP sequence when the volume of the acardiac twin is large.

415
00:56:29,419 --> 00:56:37,781
In a series from the North American Fetal Therapy Network, the median size of the acardius relative to the pump twin was 90 percent .

416
00:56:37,781 --> 00:56:49,881
Considering procedure-related risks, expectant management with close fetal surveillance is often considered if the estimated weight of the acardius is below 50 percent of the estimated weight of the pump twin .

417
00:56:49,881 --> 00:57:00,544
Intracardiac Catheter Procedures Selected fetal cardiac lesions may worsen during gestation, further complicating and even limiting options for postnatal repair.

418
00:57:00,544 --> 00:57:12,581
Severe narrowing of a cardiac outflow tract may result in progressive myocardial damage in utero, and a goal of fetal intervention is to permit muscle growth and preserve ventricular function.

419
00:57:12,581 --> 00:57:29,669
These innovative procedures include aortic valvuloplasty for critical aortic stenosis; atrial septoplasty for hypoplastic left heart syndrome with intact interatrial septum; and pulmonary valvuloplasty for pulmonary atresia with intact interventricular septum.

420
00:57:29,669 --> 00:57:41,444
Fetal aortic valvuloplasty is the most commonly performed cardiac procedure, accounting for 75 percent of cases reported by the International Fetal Cardiac Intervention Registry .

421
00:57:41,444 --> 00:57:49,244
It is offered for selected cases of critical aortic stenosis in which the left ventricle is either normal sized or dilated.

422
00:57:49,244 --> 00:57:55,956
The goal is to prevent progression to hypoplastic left heart and to permit postnatal biventricular repair.

423
00:57:55,956 --> 00:58:04,119
Under sonographic guidance, an 18-gauge cannula is inserted through uterus and fetal chest wall and into the left ventricle.

424
00:58:04,119 --> 00:58:12,556
Although the procedure is ideally performed percutaneously—through the maternal abdominal wall—laparotomy may be needed if the fetal

425
00:58:12,724 --> 00:58:15,161
position is unfavorable.

426
00:58:15,111 --> 00:58:26,449
The cannula tip is positioned in front of the stenotic aortic valve, and a 2.5- to 4.5-mm balloon catheter is guided into the aortic annulus and then inflated.

427
00:58:26,449 --> 00:58:36,099
Fetal bradycardia requiring treatment may complicate a third of cases, and hemopericardium requiring drainage affects approximately 20 percent .

428
00:58:36,099 --> 00:58:49,411
In a review of 108 fetuses treated with aortic valvuloplasty from 15 international centers, 75 percent survived until delivery, and biventricular repair was achieved in 32 percent .

429
00:58:49,411 --> 00:58:58,486
Friedman and coworkers (2018) from Boston Children’s Hospital have reported improved outcomes with the procedure in recent years.

430
00:58:58,486 --> 00:59:11,511
Of 52 aortic valvuloplasty procedures performed between 2009 and 2015, nearly 90 percent resulted in a live birth, and more than 50 percent achieved biventricular repair.

431
00:59:11,511 --> 00:59:20,486
Guseh and colleagues (2020) emphasize that most children with biventricular function still require postnatal cardiac procedures.

432
00:59:20,486 --> 00:59:31,736
The risk for neurodevelopmental impairment in childhood appears similar to cases treated with postnatal repair (Laraja, 2017; Moon-Grady, 2015).

433
00:59:31,736 --> 00:59:46,661
The subset of fetuses with hypoplastic left heart syndrome who also have an intact or restrictive interatrial septum have postnatal mortality rates of nearly 80 percent (Glantz, 2007; Jantzen, 2017).

434
00:59:46,661 --> 00:59:54,074
To help improve survival, fetal atrial septoplasty using a percutaneous balloon catheter has been offered.

435
00:59:54,074 --> 01:00:00,411
Atrial septal stent placement is often attempted at the time of septoplasty to ensure patency.

436
01:00:00,411 --> 01:00:10,524
Of 47 such procedures reported by the International Fetal Cardiac Intervention Registry, 35 percent of infants survived to hospital discharge .

437
01:00:10,524 --> 01:00:19,824
However, the 1-year survival rate was higher in those that had successful fetal cardiac intervention compared with those that had not undergone intervention.

438
01:00:19,824 --> 01:00:32,874
Fetal pulmonary valvuloplasty has been offered in cases of pulmonary atresia with intact interventricular septum to prevent development of hypoplastic right heart syndrome and subsequent single ventricle palliation.

439
01:00:32,874 --> 01:00:44,274
The International Fetal Cardiac Intervention Registry reported that of 58 cases of attempted pulmonary valvuloplasty, the procedure was technically successful in 70 percent .

440
01:00:44,274 --> 01:00:50,374
Those with successful in-utero procedures were twice as likely to achieve biventricular repair.

441
01:00:50,374 --> 01:01:01,699
However, when procedure-related losses were considered, survival to hospital discharge was similar to those who had not received fetal intervention, approximating 75 percent.

442
01:01:01,699 --> 01:01:06,024
Long-term benefits of the procedure have yet to be demonstrated.

443
01:01:06,024 --> 01:01:09,361
# Ex-Utero Intrapartum Treatment.

444
01:01:09,361 --> 01:01:19,736
This procedure allows the fetus to remain perfused by the placenta after being partially delivered, so that lifesaving treatment can be performed before completing the delivery.

445
01:01:19,736 --> 01:01:36,361
The technique was first developed to obtain an airway with fetal tumors involving the oropharynx and neck and is still used for this indication (Catalano, 1992; Kelly, 1990; Langer, 1992; Shamshirsaz, 2021).

446
01:01:36,361 --> 01:01:59,049
An ex-utero intrapartum treatment (EXIT) procedure is performed by a multidisciplinary team, which may include an obstetrician, maternal-fetal medicine specialist, pediatric surgeon(s), pediatric otolaryngologist, pediatric cardiologist, anesthesiologists for the mother and fetus, neonatologists, and specially trained nursing personnel.

447
01:01:59,049 --> 01:02:03,561
Components of the procedure are shown in Table 19-5 .

448
01:02:03,561 --> 01:02:06,136
Table 19-5.

449
01:02:06,136 --> 01:02:14,499
Components of the Ex-Utero Intrapartum Treatment (EXIT) Procedure Selected indications are listed in Table 19-2.

450
01:02:14,499 --> 01:02:24,036
EXIT is the preferred procedure for intrapartum management of large venolymphatic malformations of the neck such as the one shown in Figure 19-9.

451
01:02:24,036 --> 01:02:35,436
Criteria for EXIT with a cervical venolymphatic malformation include compression, deviation, or obstruction of the airway by the mass, and also involvement of the floor of the mouth .

452
01:02:35,436 --> 01:02:45,086
However, a review of 112 pregnancies with fetal cervical venolymphatic malformations found that only about 10 percent met these criteria.

453
01:02:45,086 --> 01:02:57,511
Other indications for EXIT include severe micrognathia and congenital high airway obstruction sequence (CHAOS), which are discussed in Chapter 15 (Figs. 15-27 and

454
01:02:57,676 --> 01:03:01,451
15-35, p. 285).

455
01:03:01,401 --> 01:03:15,201
Criteria for an EXIT procedure for micrognathia include a fetal jaw measurement below the 5th percentile along with indirect evidence of obstruction, such as hydramnios, an absent stomach bubble, or glossoptosis .

456
01:03:15,201 --> 01:03:21,776
Case selection for EXIT procedures is generally based on fetal magnetic resonance imaging findings.

457
01:03:21,776 --> 01:03:24,251
Figure 19-9.

458
01:03:24,251 --> 01:03:30,526
Ex-utero intrapartum treatment (EXIT) procedure for a venolymphatic malformation.

459
01:03:30,526 --> 01:03:45,301
A. Upon delivery of the head, placental circulation was maintained, and an airway was established over the course of 20 minutes by a team of pediatric subspecialists that included a surgeon, anesthesiologist, and otolaryngologist.

460
01:03:45,301 --> 01:03:53,401
B. Following a controlled intubation, the fetus was ready for delivery and transfer to the neonatal intensive care unit team.

461
01:03:53,401 --> 01:03:59,226
Reproduced with permission from Drs. Stacey Thomas and Patricia Santiago-Muñoz.

462
01:03:59,226 --> 01:04:04,938
In some cases, an EXIT procedure has been used as a bridge to other procedures.

463
01:04:04,938 --> 01:04:13,988
For example, resection of large thoracic masses may be accomplished by fetal thoracotomy performed with intact placental circulation.

464
01:04:13,988 --> 01:04:30,001
In a series of 16 fetuses with CCAM volume ratios >1.6 or hydrops, all of whom had mediastinal compression, Cass and colleagues (2013) reported that nine infants undergoing EXIT-to-resection survived.

465
01:04:30,001 --> 01:04:35,288
In contrast, there were no survivors with urgent postnatal surgery alone.

466
01:04:35,288 --> 01:04:44,588
Similarly, Moldenhauer (2013) reported that 20 of 22 newborns treated with EXIT-to-resection for lung masses survived.

467
01:04:44,588 --> 01:04:55,751
The EXIT procedure has also been used as a bridge to extracorporeal membrane oxygenation—EXIT-toECMO—in pregnancies with severe congenital diaphragmatic hernia.

468
01:04:55,751 --> 01:05:07,301
However, it has not been found to clearly confer survival benefit in such cases (Morris, 2009a; Shieh, 2017; Stoffan, 2012).

469
01:05:07,301 --> 01:05:27,188
Counseling prior to an EXIT procedure includes procedure-related risks such as hemorrhage from placental abruption or uterine atony, need for cesarean delivery in future pregnancies, higher risk for subsequent uterine rupture or dehiscence, possible need for hysterectomy, and fetal death or permanent neonatal disability.

470
01:05:27,188 --> 01:05:41,576
Compared with cesarean delivery, the EXIT procedure is associated with greater blood loss, a higher incidence of wound complications, and a longer operating time (Noah, 2002; Shamshirsaz, 2019).

